---
import CaseStudyLayout from '../../layouts/CaseStudyLayout.astro';
import ImagePlaceholder from '../../components/ImagePlaceholder.astro';
import { getProject } from '../../data/projects';
import { url } from '../../lib/paths';

const project = getProject('douglas-county-housing-partnership');

const sections = [
  { id: 'overview', label: 'Overview' },
  { id: 'challenge', label: 'Challenge' },
  { id: 'role', label: 'My role' },
  { id: 'approach', label: 'Approach' },
  { id: 'execution', label: 'Execution' },
  { id: 'screens', label: 'The site' },
  { id: 'outcome', label: 'Outcome' },
  { id: 'why-it-matters', label: 'Why it matters' },
];
---

<CaseStudyLayout
  project={project}
  sections={sections}
>
  <section class="cs-section" id="overview">
    <h2>Overview</h2>
    <p>
      Douglas County Housing Partnership needed a digital presence that could explain what the
      organization does — programs, resources, housing-related services and organizational
      information — to people who arrive with a specific need and limited patience. I took the project
      from strategy through implementation: information architecture, user pathways, page structure,
      SEO, and the build itself.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="cs-section" id="challenge">
    <h2>Challenge</h2>
    <p>
      Housing organizations carry a hard content problem. The audience isn't one group: it includes
      residents looking for help, partner organizations, property owners, funders and board members.
      The information is dense, partly regulatory, and often the thing a visitor needs most is three
      clicks away from the thing the organization most wants to say.
    </p>
    <p>Specifically, the site had to:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Make programs and services findable without requiring prior knowledge of how housing programs are organized</li>
      <li>Separate "I need help now" pathways from organizational and partner-facing content</li>
      <li>Present resources in a structure that holds up as the organization adds to it</li>
      <li>Be credible — a housing nonprofit's website is part of how it establishes trust</li>
      <li>Be discoverable in search for the local, need-driven queries people actually use</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="cs-section" id="role">
    <h2>My role</h2>
    <p>
      This was a freelance engagement where I owned the full scope rather than one slice of it:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Digital strategy</strong> — what the site needed to accomplish, and for whom</li>
      <li><strong>Information architecture</strong> — the page hierarchy and navigation model</li>
      <li><strong>UX decisions</strong> — pathways, page-level structure, calls to action</li>
      <li><strong>Content organization</strong> — grouping and sequencing existing material into pages that make sense to a first-time visitor</li>
      <li><strong>SEO</strong> — page titles, metadata, heading structure, internal linking, image optimization, local search considerations</li>
      <li><strong>Design and development</strong> — the custom site, built and QA'd</li>
      <li><strong>AI-assisted development</strong> — used where it shortened implementation without compromising the result</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="cs-section" id="approach">
    <h2>Approach</h2>

    <h3>1. Define the information architecture first</h3>
    <p>
      Before any design, I mapped what the organization does into a hierarchy a stranger could
      navigate — grouping by what a visitor is trying to accomplish rather than by internal
      department or program-funding structure. That decision shaped everything downstream, including
      the URL structure and the internal linking model.
    </p>

    <h3>2. Organize user pathways</h3>
    <p>
      Each primary audience got a clear entry point and a short route to the information or contact
      action that resolves their visit. Where a page served two audiences, I split it rather than
      hedging the copy.
    </p>

    <h3>3. Build a search-friendly page hierarchy</h3>
    <p>
      Pages were structured so that each one has a single clear subject, a descriptive title and
      heading, and a logical parent. That's good SEO, but it's also what makes a page extractable: an
      answer engine can lift a self-contained explanation of a program out of a page built this way.
    </p>

    <h3>4. Implement, then optimize technically</h3>
    <p>
      With the structure settled, I built the site and worked through the technical layer: semantic
      markup, metadata, heading hierarchy, descriptive alt text, internal linking, image
      optimization, and mobile behavior.
    </p>

    <h3>5. QA across desktop and mobile</h3>
    <p>
      Every template was checked at mobile, tablet and desktop widths, including navigation behavior,
      link targets, form behavior and readability at small sizes.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="cs-section" id="execution">
    <h2>Execution</h2>
    <p>What was actually delivered:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>A custom, responsive website — not a template configuration</li>
      <li>A documented page hierarchy with clean, descriptive URLs</li>
      <li>Navigation and internal linking that reflect the audience pathways rather than the org chart</li>
      <li>Page-level SEO: unique titles, meta descriptions, semantic heading structure, descriptive image alt text</li>
      <li>Content reorganized into pages with single, clear subjects</li>
      <li>Technical QA across breakpoints and primary browsers</li>
      <li>Handoff guidance so the organization can add resources without breaking the structure</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="cs-section" id="screens">
    <h2>The site</h2>
    <p class="text-muted" style="font-size:var(--step--1);">
      The live site is at
      <a href="https://douglascountyhousingpartnership.org/" rel="noopener">douglascountyhousingpartnership.org</a>.
    </p>

    <figure class="ph">
      <img
        src={url('/images/projects/dchp/homepage-desktop.webp')}
        width="800"
        height="556"
        alt="Douglas County Housing Partnership homepage at desktop width: a headline reading 'Housing Help for Douglas County Residents' over a photograph of two residents, with 'Find Housing' and 'Contact DCHP' as the two primary actions, and a 'Find What You Need' section beginning below."
        loading="lazy"
        decoding="async"
      />
      <figcaption>
        Homepage: one sentence explaining who the organization serves, and two
        clear routes — find housing, or talk to someone.
      </figcaption>
    </figure>

    <figure class="ph ph--phone">
      <img
        src={url('/images/projects/dchp/homepage-mobile.webp')}
        width="640"
        height="1109"
        alt="The same homepage at phone width: the navigation collapses to a labelled Menu button, the headline stacks over three lines, and the two primary actions become full-width buttons."
        loading="lazy"
        decoding="async"
      />
      <figcaption>
        Mobile, where most need-driven traffic arrives. The two actions become
        full-width targets and the navigation collapses behind a labelled button.
      </figcaption>
    </figure>

    <ImagePlaceholder
      label="Program page"
      description="A representative program or services page, showing the page-level content structure."
      path="/images/projects/dchp/program-page.png"
      caption="Program template: one subject per page, with a clear next action."
    />

    <ImagePlaceholder
      label="Resources section"
      description="The resources index, showing how material is grouped for scanning."
      path="/images/projects/dchp/resources.png"
      caption="Resources, grouped so the structure survives additions."
    />
  </section>

  <section class="cs-section" id="outcome">
    <h2>Outcome</h2>
    <p>
      The organization has a custom website it owns, with a structure that matches how its audiences
      actually arrive and a technical foundation that doesn't need to be redone to support search.
      Capabilities delivered:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>A navigable, audience-first information architecture replacing an ad hoc structure</li>
      <li>Search-ready page hierarchy, metadata and internal linking implemented at launch rather than retrofitted</li>
      <li>A responsive experience QA'd across devices</li>
      <li>A content model the organization can extend without a redesign</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Quantitative performance data for this engagement:
      <span class="todo">[ADD VERIFIED RESULT]</span> — organic search, engagement or inquiry metrics
      to be added once confirmed from the organization's analytics.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="cs-section" id="why-it-matters">
    <h2>Why it matters</h2>
    <p>
      This is the project I point to when the question is whether I can go from strategy to a shipped
      build. The architecture work and the implementation weren't two engagements with a handoff in
      between — which is the main reason the structure survived into the finished site intact.
    </p>
    <p>
      It also reflects how I think about SEO on a small site: there's no budget for a content program
      that out-publishes competitors, so the advantage has to come from clarity. A site where every
      page has one subject, a descriptive title and a sensible parent is both easier for people to
      use and easier for search engines and answer engines to interpret correctly.
    </p>
  </section>
</CaseStudyLayout>
